@page "/projects/whereisfood"

<ProjectPage Title="WhereIsFood"
             Kicker="In progress · 2026"
             AccentClass="accent-red"
             Summary="A live map of Gothenburg's food trucks. Trucks broadcast their location three different ways; the map works out how much to trust each one and says so in plain language."
             Tech="@(new[] { "TypeScript", "Next.js 16", "React 19", "Supabase Postgres", "MapLibre GL JS", "Protomaps", "Tailwind CSS v4", "Vitest" })"
             Links="@(new[] { new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Source on GitHub", "https://github.com/Ollie1994/whereisfood") })">

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        Gothenburg has had no central live food-truck map since Streetkäk shut down in
        2017. WhereIsFood fills that gap: open it with no login and see which trucks are
        out right now, where they are, and what they're serving. Consumers are always
        anonymous — no account, no tracking — and the interface is in Swedish.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Phases 1 and 2 of 8 are complete.</strong> The map renders and both
        ingestion lanes are built and verified end-to-end against a local Supabase stack.
        The map isn't wired to live data yet — that's phase 4.
    </p>

    <h2>Three ways in, weighted by trust</h2>

    <p>
        Food truck owners don't want another dashboard to log into, so the system meets
        them where they already post. Location updates arrive three ways, and each carries
        a different <strong>source confidence</strong>:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Dashboard</strong> — an authenticated owner entering structured data.
            Trusted completely; no parsing involved.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Webhook</strong> — a Make.com automation forwards a social post
            caption, which goes through the parser.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inbound email</strong> — the truck emails an address; the parser runs
            on the body. Least trusted, because anyone can send email.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        A manual post always overrides a parsed one covering the same time window.
    </p>

    <h2>Confidence users can actually read</h2>

    <p>
        Trust is scored on two axes — <code>parser confidence × source confidence</code> —
        but that number never reaches the user. Showing someone "0.68" tells them nothing.
        It surfaces as marker colour and a sentence instead: <em>"Posted by truck today"</em>
        against <em>"Based on this morning's post"</em>.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            The decision I'd defend hardest is showing <strong>absence honestly</strong>. A
            truck with no update today appears grey at its last known spot, explicitly
            labelled as stale, rather than being hidden or silently shown as if it were
            current. Hiding it makes the map look better and makes it useless — you'd
            arrive at an empty street.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>A Swedish-language parser built as pure functions</h2>

    <p>
        Captions and emails run through a dictionary-based pipeline: normalise, detect
        negation (<code>inställt</code>, <code>stängt</code>), extract location, resolve
        the date (<code>idag</code>, <code>imorgon</code>, weekday names — always to the
        nearest <em>future</em> day), extract a time range, then score confidence.
    </p>

    <p>
        There is <strong>no <code>new Date()</code> anywhere inside the parser</strong> —
        the current time is passed in. That one rule is what makes the whole thing
        testable: "what does <em>imorgon</em> mean" is a question with a fixed answer only
        if you control what "now" is. Raw posts are also retained as a training corpus for
        a future machine-learning parser intended to replace the dictionary approach.
    </p>

    <h2>Cost and security decisions</h2>

    <p>
        Map tiles are the usual hidden cost in a mapping product — hosted tile APIs bill
        per request and lock you in. The map serves <strong>self-hosted Protomaps vector
        tiles</strong> through MapLibre GL JS instead, so traffic growth doesn't create a
        bill and the provider can be changed at will.
    </p>

    <p>
        Both ingestion endpoints are publicly reachable, so they verify their source with
        <strong>HMAC-SHA256 and constant-time comparison</strong> — a naive string compare
        leaks information about the secret through timing. Input goes through pure
        validators before anything is persisted, and the database uses row-level security
        with least-privilege grants. Endpoints return <code>200</code> immediately and
        defer the write, so a slow database can't make the caller time out and retry.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>

    <p>
        The architecture is strictly layered — HTTP routes, then services, then the
        database, with parsers and validators as pure functions at the edge. Covered by a
        Vitest unit suite and verified end-to-end against a local Supabase stack running in
        Docker. I run the project through a structured, phased, issue- and PR-driven
        process with custom workflow rules I wrote myself.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
